  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong high pressure building into the Mid-Atlantic \nand low pressure well off the FL coast will result in a period \nof continued large waves across the OBX. The high waves combined\nwith King Tides this week will lead to additional minor coastal\nflooding impacts.\n\nFor Hatteras Island, seas offshore are in the 6-8 foot range, \nslowly subsiding through the coming week, but long period \nswells will continue to cause issues for vulnerable areas. For \nocean-side: high tide is between 0800-0830 PM EDT today. \nSoundside high tides lag by up to 3 hours behind the oceanside \ntides. The National Parks Service and NCDOT have reported \nsubstantial beach erosion from south Nags Head to Buxton, along \nwith significant overwash occurring on side streets in Rodanthe.\nDue to hazardous road conditions and flooding, NC-12 remains \nclosed from the Marc Basnight Bridge to Rodanthe. \n\nCoastal flooding will be likely well into this week despite the\ndiminishing of winds, with \"sunny day\" flooding across much of \nthe tidal regions of ENC around times of high tide, then slowly\nimprove as the King Tides ease later in the week and into the\nweekend. Coastal flood advisories continue through the high tide\ncycle on Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pleasantly cooler and drier air mass will \nremain with us through early this week, with near climo highs, \nto slightly below avg lows. Lows getting well down into the 40s\nwith some 30s again possible when the high pressure ridges into\nENC allowing for excellent rad cooling under clear skies, calm \nwinds, and low TD's. Low end fog threat exists with the\naforementioned conditions, with best chances east of hwy 17.\nKept fog out of the forecast for now given the low probs, but\nmay be added with later updates. Hts/thicknesses begin to build\nby mid week, with temps climbing back above climo with highs in\nthe mid 70s, then upper 70s by Thursday into the weekend. Lows \nwill remain closer to climo (50s) as dry airmass remains with a \nlarge diurnal temp range.",
